KUBERNETES COMMANDS :
===================

A. Cluster health and basic information

1. Check the client and server version

  Command : kubectl version

Shows the client version and kubernetes API version

2. Check Cluster information

Command : kubectl cluster-info

Shows the Kubernetes control plan endpoints and other cluster services

3. Check the nodes

Command : kubectl get nodes

4. Detailed node information 

Command : kubectl get nodes -o wide

5. inspect one node 

Command : kubectl describe node <node-name>

Example : kubectl describe node k8s-node1

6. Check the System pods

Command : kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide

7. Check All namaspaces

Command : kubectl get namespaces

8. Check the resources across all namespaces

Command : kubectl get pods -A

9. Create namespaces

Command : kubectl create namespace <name>

Example : kubectl create namespace kube-lab

10. Set the current namespace

Command : kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=kube-lab

Makes k8s-lab the default namespace for subsequent kubectl commands using the current context.

11. Check the current namespace 

Command : kubectl config view --minify


12. Get the pods from specific namespaces

Command : kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Example : kubectl get pods -n default

13. Create pod

Command : kubectl run <pod-name> --image=<image-name>

Example : kubectl run nginx-pod --image=nginx

14. Create pod using spcific namspace

Command : kubectl run pod <pod-name> --image=<name> -n <namespace>

Example : kubectl run pod nginx-pod --image=nginx:latest -n kube-lab


15. Check  the pods

Command : kubectl get pods

Detailed information about pod 

Command : kubectl get pods -o wide

16. Check the pod with specific namespace

Command : kubectl pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o wide

Example : kubectl pod nginx-pod -n kube-lab -o wide

17. Inspect the pod with specific name space

Command : kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Example : kubectl describe pod nginx-pod -n kube-lab 

18. Get Detailed information about pod

Command : kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

Example : kubectl get pod nginx-pod

19. Check the pod logs

Command : kubectl logs <pod-name>

Example : kubectl logs nginx-pod

20. Check logs with namespace

Command : kubectl logs nginx-pod -n kube-lab

21. Follow logs continuesly

Command : kubectl logs -f <pod-name> -n kube-lab

Example : kubectl logs -f nginx-pod -n kube-lab

Exit : Ctrl + c

22.Enter inside the container

Command : kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- /bin/bash

Example : kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- /bin/bash

23.Enter inside the container with namespace

Command : kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <name-space> -- /bin/bash

Example : kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -n kube-lab -- /bin/bash

24.Check the specific pod ip

Command : kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o wide

Example : kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o wide

25. Test the application inside the pod

Command : kubectl exec <pod-name> -- <image-name> -v

Example : kubectl exec nginx-pod -- nginx -v

26. Delete the pod

Command : kubectl delete pod <pod-name>

Example : kubectl delete pod nginx-pod

27. Delete pod inside the namespaces

Command : kubectl delete pod <pod-name> -n <name-space>

Example : kubectl delete pod nginx-pod -n kube-lab

