### Service

A Kubernetes Service is an object that provides stable network endpoints to access a group of pods

1. Stable Ip address : If pod changes ips ,service provides a stable virtual ip

2. Stable DNS name : Kubernets creates DNS records for service, Application can use the service name

3. Traffic forwarding : Service forwards the traffic to matching pods

4. Load Distribution : When Multiple pods behind the service , Traffic can be distributed among them

5. Pod Discovery :  service use labels and selectors to identify which pods should recieve traffic

62. Syntax : kubectl expose deployment <depolyment-name> --type=<service-type> --port=<service-port> --target-port=<container-port> -n <namespace>

Example : kubectl expose deployment  web-app --name=web-app-clusterip --type=Cluster-IP --port=80 --target-port=80 -n dev-lab

ClusterIP is used to expose an application internally within the Kubernetes cluster. It provides a stable IP and DNS name and routes traffic to the Pods selected by the Service

63. See the actual Servuce ip address

 Command : kubectl get svc -A

64. See the service in the namespace 

 Syntax : kubectl get service -n <namespace>

 Command : kubectl get service -n dev-lab

 65. Inspect the which pods to sends the traffic to 

  Syntax: kubectl describe service <deployment-name> -n dev-lab

  Example : kubectl describe service web-app -n dev-lab

 66. See the end points directly

  Command : kubectl get endpoints <deployment-name> -n <namespace>

  Exmple  : kubectl get endpoints web-app -n dev-lab

  Endpoints represent the network addresses of the Pods that are selected by a Service. They tell the Service where to forward incoming traffic.

 ## NodePort  : NodePort is a Kubernetes Service type that exposes a Service externally by opening a specific port on every Kubernetes Node. External clients can access the application using the Node IP and NodePort. It is commonly used for testing, development, and environments where a LoadBalancer is not available.  port = 3000 --> 32767

 Syntax : kubectl expose deployment <deployment> --type=NodePort --port=<service-port> --type-port=<container-port> -n <namespace>

 Example : kubectl expose deployment web-app -name web-app-nodeport --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=80 -n dev-lab

 Check the nodeport 

 kubectl get svc -n dev-lab 


 Verify the complete Network connection

 Command : kubectl describe svc web-app-nodeport -n dev-lab



 ### ConfigMap:

 A ConfigMap is a Kubernetes object used to store non-sensitive configuration data as key-value pairs. It allows us to separate application configuration from the container image and inject the configuration into Pods.

 --> ConfigMap is an independent Kubernetes resource. A Pod does not create a ConfigMap. A Pod consumes or references a ConfigMap.

 Basic Configmap Synatx :

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap

metadata:
  name: app-config
  namespace: kube-lab

data:
  APP_ENV: "development"
  APP_PORT: "80"
  LOG_LEVEL: "info"


67. See the Configmaps 

  kubectl get configmap <name> -n <namespace>

  Example : kubectl get configmap app-map -n kube-lab

69. Verify the actual values 

 kubectl describe configmap <name> -n <namespace>

 Example : kubectl describe configmap app-map -n kube-lab

 Synatx : 

 kubectl create config <name> -n <namespace> --from-literal=<key>=<value>

for multiple values :

 kubectl create configmap <configmap-name> -n <namespace> --from-literal=<key>=<value>  --from-literal=<key>=<value>

 Example : kubectl create configmap app-config -n kube-lab --from-literal=APP_ENV=development --from-literal=APP_PORT=80 --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=info

configmaps method : 

1. configMapKeyRef : 

 configMapKeyRef is used to retrieve a specific key from a ConfigMap and expose its value as an environment variable inside a container.
 
--> it is use ful when we want to access one specific key

   env:
        - name: APP_ENV
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: app-map
              key: APP_ENV


              
2. envFrom : 

envFrom loads all key-value pairs from a ConfigMap as environment variables inside the container.

Synatax : 

envFrom:
  - configMapRef:
      name: app-map

3. Mount ConfigMap as files inside the container:

a. volumes : which config map to use

volumes:
  - name: app-config-volume
    configMap:
      name: app-map

b. volumeMounts: 

volumeMounts:
  - name: app-config-volume
    mountPath: /etc/app-config


Check the values : kubectl exec nginx -n kube-lab -- cat /etc/app-config/APP_ENV


### Secret :

A Kubernetes Secret is an object used to store sensitive information such as passwords, tokens, usernames, and TLS credentials. Pods can consume Secrets as environment variables or mounted files.

For Exmple :

Username
Password
API Token
SSH key
TLS certificate

 --> Kubernetes Secret is designed to hold sensitive data, and Secret data is base64-encoded in the manifest/API representation; encryption at rest can be configured separately

70. Create a Kubernetes Secret :

Syntax : kubectl create secret generic <secret-name> -n <namespace> --from-literals=<key>=<value> --form-literals=<key>=<value>

Example : kubectl create secret generic db-secret -n kube-lab --from-literal=DB_USERNAME=admin --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=admin@123

71. Verify 

  Syntax : kubectl get secret <name> -n <namespace>
  

  Example : kubectl get secret db-secret -n kube-lab


  --> Opaque is the default type for a generic Kubernetes Secret used to store arbitrary key-value data such as usernames, passwords, and tokens.


72. inspect the secret 

  kubectl describe secret <name> -n <namespace>

  Example : kubectl describe secret db-secret -n kube-lab


Manifest file structure :

 apiVersion: v1
 kind: Secret

 metadata:
   name: <secret-name>
   namespace: <namespace>
 
 type: Opaque

 data:
  <key>: <base64-value>


  --> secretKeyRef : secretKeyRef is used to inject a specific key from a Kubernetes Secret into a container as an environment variable.


   syntax : 

   env:
     -name: <environment-variable-name>
     valueFrom:
        secretkeyRef: 
           name: <secret-name>
           key: <secret-key>


  Example : 

   env:
        - name: DB_USER
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: db-secret-manifest
              key: DB_USERNAME

        - name: DB_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: db-secret-manifest
              key: DB_PASSWORD

  
  inspect : kubectl exec secret-pod -n kube-lab -- env | grep DB_

-- > envFrom : envFrom is used when you want to load all key-value pairs from a Secret into the container as environment variables.


   Syntax : 

   envFrom:
  - secretRef:
      name: <secret-name>


  Example : 

  envFrom:
  - secretRef:
      name: db-secret-manifest

--> Volume : A Kubernetes Secret can be mounted as a volume, where each Secret key is exposed as a file inside the container.

Syntax :
   
   volumeMounts:
  - name: <volume-name>
    mountPath: <path>

volumes:
  - name: <volume-name>
    secret:
      secretName: <secret-name>
  
Example :

volumeMounts:
  - name: secret-volume
    mountPath: /etc/db-secret

volumes:
  - name: secret-volume
    secret:
      secretName: db-secret-manifest











##LoadBalencer :
---------------
A LoadBalancer is a Kubernetes Service type used to expose an application outside the cluster through an external load balancer.

MetalLB : MetalLB is a load-balancer implementation for bare-metal Kubernetes clusters that provides external IP addresses for LoadBalancer Services.

Kubernetes
   ↓
No cloud provider
   ↓
LoadBalancer Service
   ↓
EXTERNAL-IP: <pending>

MetalLB has two main parts

1. Controller :

The controller watches Kubernetes resources and handles allocation of IP addresses to LoadBalancer Services.

2.Speaker :

The speaker runs on cluster nodes and announces the assigned IP address to the network.


             MetalLB
          ┌───────────┐
          │ Controller│
          └─────┬─────┘
                │
                ↓
        Assign external IP
                │
                ↓
          LoadBalancer
            Service
                │
                ↓
          MetalLB Speaker
                │
                ↓
             Network






  










 
















